  "worked_solution": "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y & 3{y}^{2}-6x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-6y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-6x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-8x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=2\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=6x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=-6 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=-6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=0\\cdot 0-(-6)^{2}=-36<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(2,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,2)=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,2)=det({f}^{″}(2,2))=12\\cdot 12-(-6)^{2}=108>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(2,2)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,2)=({f}^{″}(2,2))_{11}=12>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(2,2).$",
